        <p class="profile-detail-region">Ideology · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Pan-Slavism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1848 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val">Ján Kollár, a Slovak poet who saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> as one nation of four tribes; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frantisek-palacky">František Palacký</a>, the Czech historian who presided over the 1848 congress; Pavel Jozef Šafárik, a Slovak philologist; Ľudovít Štúr, who turned towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; Juraj Križanić, a 17th-century Croatian missionary counted among the first pan-Slavists; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-garrigue-masaryk">Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk</a>, who completed the Euro-Slavist programme</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Key texts</span><span class="info-stat-val">Štúr’s Das Slawenthum und die Welt der Zukunft (Slavdom and the World of the Future), written in the early 1850s and first published in 1867 in a Russian translation; Masaryk’s New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>: Slavic Viewpoint; the anthem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hey-slavs">Hey, Slavs</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Core ideas</span><span class="info-stat-val">the unity of the Slavic peoples; a shared Slavic identity and ancestry drawn from history, philology and folklore; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> as one nation of four tribes; Slavic autonomy within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy (Austroslavism); union under Russian leadership; a South Slavic nation of three faiths; Euro-Slavism within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">Pan-Slavism has little public following; parties that include it in their programmes live on the fringe or, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, in a controlled opposition, and apart from cultural and heritage organisations no form of union among Slavic countries exists</span></div>
