    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Pan-Slavism grew from Romantic nationalism after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, when Slavic scholars of history, philology and folklore encouraged a sense of shared identity and ancestry.</li><li>Slavic thinkers disagreed about its aim: Palacký sought Slavic autonomy inside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, Štúr proposed union under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and most Serbian intellectuals wanted a South Slavic state.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> largely rejected it as serving Russian interests, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> treated it as a reactionary idea of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, so the ideal faded after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frantisek-palacky">František Palacký</a> was asked in 1848 to declare for German national unity, he replied that if <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> had not long existed, it would be necessary in the interest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and of humanity itself to create it.</p></aside>
    </section>

