    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1815</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> end, and the Pan-Slavism movement begins to grow rapidly</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1848</span><span class="rf-text">The First Pan-Slav Congress meets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> from 2 to 16 June and is broken up when the army <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombard">bombards</a> the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1849</span><span class="rf-text">The Slovak National Council petitions Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a> to recognise the Slovak nation within defined borders</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1863</span><span class="rf-text">The Association of Serbian Philology commemorates the death of Cyril a thousand years earlier</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1867</span><span class="rf-text">A Slavic Congress meets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, boycotted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1918</span><span class="rf-text">The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> unites most South Slavic-speaking nations under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karadordevic-dynasty">Karađorđević dynasty</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1948</span><span class="rf-text">The split between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> and Stalin ends the proposals to bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> into a Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-gundulic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-gundulic.jpg" alt="Ivan Gundulić" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-gundulic">Ivan Gundulić</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_Gondola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonymous, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pan-Slavism emerged in the mid-19th century, much like Pan-Germanism. Both movements flourished from the sense of unity and nationalism felt by members of many European ethnic groups after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, as a pushback against traditional European monarchies. As in other Romantic nationalist movements, Slavic intellectuals and scholars in the developing fields of history, philology and folklore encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>’ interest in their shared identity and ancestry. Its common symbols were the Pan-Slavic colours of blue, white and red and the anthem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hey-slavs">Hey, Slavs</a>.</p><p>The first pan-Slavists named are the 16th-century Croatian writer Vinko Pribojević, the Dalmatian Aleksandar Komulović (1548–1608), the Croat Bartol Kašić (1575–1650), the Ragusan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-gundulic">Ivan Gundulić</a> (1589–1638) and the Croatian Catholic missionary Juraj Križanić (c. 1618 – 1683). Tomasz Kamusella attributes early manifestations of Pan-Slavic thought within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy to the Slovaks Adam Franz Kollár (1718–1783) and Pavel Jozef Šafárik (1795–1861).</p><p>The movement grew rapidly after the end of the Napoleonic Wars in 1815. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> of 1814–1815, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>’s representative, Prince von Metternich, detected a threat to the pre-war order in nationalists’ demands for independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/frantisek-palacky"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frantisek-palacky.jpg" alt="František Palacký" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/frantisek-palacky">František Palacký</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jan_Vil%C3%ADmek_-_Franti%C5%A1ek_Palack%C3%BD_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jan Vilímek, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pan-Slavism held that the Slavic peoples shared an identity and ancestry and should be united. The Slovak poet Ján Kollár held that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> formed one nation divided into four “tribes”, each with its own literary language: Czechoslovak, Polish, Russian (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-slavs">East Slavs</a>) and Illyrian (South Slavs). Andrej Ľudovít Radlinský held a similar view.</p><p>Thinkers differed on what unity meant. The Austroslav <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frantisek-palacky">František Palacký</a> called for the cooperation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and endorsed the Habsburg monarchy as the political formation most likely to protect the peoples of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Ľudovít Štúr, who had codified the first widely used standard Slovak language, turned away from Austroslavism in Das Slawenthum und die Welt der Zukunft. He wrote that “every nation has its time under God’s sun”, with the linden, a symbol of the Slavs, blossoming while the oak, a symbol of the Teutons, had bloomed long ago. He saw the Slavs as a tree whose “minor” nations were branches and whose trunk was Russian, and proposed that they unite under Tsarist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, adopt Russian as their common literary language and convert to Orthodoxy.</p><p>Most Serbian intellectuals sought to unite all the South Slavs, Catholic, Muslim and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, as a “Southern-Slavic nation of three faiths”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i.jpg" alt="Franz Joseph I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franz_Joseph_I_of_Austria.1910_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carl Pietzner, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The First Pan-Slav Congress was held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> from 2 to 16 June 1848, during the revolutionary movement of that year. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> had refused to send representatives to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> Assembly, feeling that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> had a distinct interest from the Germans. Palacký presided, and most delegates were Czech and Slovak. The delegates were both anti-Austrian and anti-Russian. The moderately liberal “Right”, led by Palacký and Šafárik, favoured Slav autonomy within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy; the radical “Left”, led by Karel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabina">Sabina</a>, Josef Václav Frič and Karol Libelt, pressed for a close alliance with the revolutionary-democratic movements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. When an uprising broke out in Prague in mid-June, Field Marshal Windischgrätz bombarded the city, the insurgents surrendered, and the military authorities expelled the delegates.</p><p>In 1849 the Slovak National Council petitioned Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a> to recognise the Slovak nation within defined borders, and he replied only with a general reference to the equality of all his peoples. A Slavic Congress met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1867, three years after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> crushed the January Uprising, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> boycotted it.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> the South Slavs were divided among several entities, and Austroslavism was a strong alternative to Pan-Slavism, especially among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito.jpg" alt="Josip Broz Tito" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Josip_Broz_Tito_uniform_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pan-Slavism was never the doctrine of a state in the way some ideologies were, but states used it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> often claimed Pan-Slavism as a justification for its moves in the Balkan Peninsula against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, which had held Slavic lands for centuries; the Balkan campaign that followed is described as freeing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> from Ottoman rule with Russian help and initiative.</p><p>Because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> were dispersed among several regions and had ties to the independent Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, they were among the strongest supporters of South Slavic independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> and of a common state under the Serbian monarchy. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karadordevic-dynasty">Karađorđević dynasty</a>, united most South Slavic-speaking nations regardless of religion, but not the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> there were proposals to bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> into a Greater Yugoslavia, abandoned after the split between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> in 1948. Tito, premier of a socialist Yugoslavia and its president from 1953, promoted “Brotherhood and Unity” among its peoples.</p><p>In communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> the USSR used Pan-Slavism as a propaganda tool to justify its control. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> itself Pan-Slavists faced persecution during the Stalinist repressions (the Slavists case).</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> feared that Pan-Slavists would endanger the empire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, who had a strong tradition of statehood, were alarmed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>’s Pan-Slavic rhetoric, and many saw Pan-Slavism as serving Russian interests. Polish émigré thinkers nevertheless spoke of the freedom of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>. The Poles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> instead embraced the wide autonomy their province gained, and in December 1866 the Galician Diet assured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a> of its loyalty on condition that autonomy and national rights were respected. A Pan-Slavic federation was proposed on the condition that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> be excluded. After 1918 no faction in independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> considered Pan-Slavism a serious alternative, viewing it as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a>, and it is widely seen there as an ideology of Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a>.</p><p>Bolshevik teaching viewed Pan-Slavism as a reactionary element associated with the Russian Empire and contrary to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a>. Dimitrije Matić, president of the Association of Serbian Philology, spoke in 1863 of an “ethnically pure” Slavonic people of “purely Slavonic faces and of purely Slavonic character”.</p><p>Relations among Slavic states also strained: border clashes over Silesia led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-czechoslovak-war">Polish–Czechoslovak War</a>, and tensions between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> and Slovaks appeared before and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tomas-garrigue-masaryk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tomas-garrigue-masaryk.jpg" alt="Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tomas-garrigue-masaryk">Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tom%C3%A1%C5%A1_Garrigue_Masaryk_1925.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The old ideals were left behind early. The creation of an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> made them anachronistic, and the maxim “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> and Trianon have put an end to all Slavisms” described the authentic idea of Slavic unity as all but gone after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> the Slavic peoples were united under Soviet dominance, yet Pan-Slavism was rejected as reactionary to communist ideals, and the unity was largely put to rest by the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> in the late 1980s and the breakup of Czechoslovakia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>.</p><p>Euro-Slavism grew from the idea that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-integration">European integration</a> would solve the issues of Slavic peoples. Its origins date to 1846, when Karel Havlíček Borovský first proposed it; Palacký refined it into a provisional political programme and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-garrigue-masaryk">Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk</a> completed it in New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>: Slavic Viewpoint. Euro-Slavists oppose both Russophilia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> under Russian domination, and it is considered a modern form of Austro-Slavist and Neo-Slavist movements.</p><p>Parties with Pan-Slavism in their programmes are usually on the fringe, among them, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, People’s Party – Our Slovakia. The Russo-Ukrainian War has had a divisive role, and pro-Russian sentiment has become less popular. Similarities among the Slavic languages have inspired constructed Pan-Slavic languages, of which Interslavic is the most prominent.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/hey-slavs">Hey, Slavs<span>1834</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Pan-Slavism was a political ideology of the mid-19th century that stressed the unity of the Slavic peoples. Its thinkers split between autonomy under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, union under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and a South Slavic state; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> distrusted it, and little of it survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> and Euro-Slavism.</em></p>

